\DOC LOCAL_CMSUBC9_P521_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_CMSUBC9_P521_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
LOCAL_CMSUBC9_P521_TAC advances an {eventually}-style machine-execution goal
past one inlined subroutine block, replacing the current state variable by a
fresh successor state and recording the block's arithmetic postcondition as a
new assumption.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} offset/index used to locate the code block to be
executed; the second is a {string list} naming the variables of the block's
specification — the first names the result value written by the block, and the
remaining ones name its input values, which appear in the newly added
arithmetic assumption.
{
   A ?- eventually ... s
   ======================================  LOCAL_CMSUBC9_P521_TAC n ["t"; "x1"; ...; "xn"]
   A' ?- eventually ... s'
}
\noindent Here {s'} is a new state variable. Every assumption of {A} mentioning
the old state — program counter, loaded-bytes, register and memory-contents
readings, and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation from the initial state — is
removed and re-asserted about {s'}, with the program counter advanced and the
memory assumption for the result location now equating it to the result
variable {t} rather than its previous contents. {A'} also contains one new
assumption: an implication whose hypotheses bound the input values {x1 ... xn}
and whose conclusion gives the modular-arithmetic value of {t} in terms of
them. Assumptions that do not refer to the machine state (nonoverlapping facts
and earlier arithmetic implications) are carried through unchanged, and the
conclusion is unchanged apart from the new state variable.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of transformation, on goals of
this one shape, always with the same argument values.

\ENDDOC
